
## Installing Git

- I can check whether Git is installed with:

    git --version

- If it is installed, I should get something similar to:

    git version 2.x.x

- On macOS I can install Git using Homebrew if necessary:

    brew install git

- On Windows I can install Git through Git for Windows.

- After installing Git, I should configure my name and email.

    git config --global user.name "My Name"

    git config --global user.email "myemail@example.com"

- These details are attached to the commits I make.

---

## Git Repository

- A Git repository is a project that Git is tracking.

- I can turn a normal project folder into a Git repository using:

    git init

- This creates a hidden `.git` folder.

- The `.git` folder contains the information Git needs to track my project.

- I normally should not manually edit the `.git` folder.

- Example:

    my-project/
        main.py
        README.md
        .git/

- Once `.git` exists, Git can track changes inside that project.

---

## Checking Git Status

- One of the most useful commands is:

    git status

- It tells me what is happening in my repository.

- It can tell me:
    - Which files have changed
    - Which files are untracked
    - Which files are staged
    - Whether I have commits waiting to be pushed
    - Which branch I am currently on

- I should get into the habit of using:

    git status

  regularly.

